WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.798
The world of "One Piece" is finally coming
to live-action in the form of a Netflix television show.

00:00:05.798 --> 00:00:09.180
But who's behind bringing this worldwide phenomenon
to life?

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.456
Here's what you need to know before you watch
Netflix's "One Piece."

00:00:12.821 --> 00:00:18.029
News of an American-made, live-action "One
Piece" TV show dates back to July 2017.

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:22.010
This was when producer and self-professed
"One Piece" fan Marty Adelstein announced

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:26.980
that his company Tomorrow Studios had secured
a partnership with Shueisha and author Eiichiro

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.439
Oda to adapt "One Piece" into a live-action
TV series.

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:34.860
Oda later revealed that he actually entered
discussions about a live-action, English language

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:40.490
version of "One Piece" a year earlier in 2016,
so it's been a long time coming at this point.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.620
"So what do you say?

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.810
Are you with me?"

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:49.810
Netflix officially picked up the series in
2020 and ordered a 10-episode first season

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:54.983
that was later reduced to eight, though production
faced lengthy delays during the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:00:54.983 --> 00:01:01.469
Production picked up again during 2021 and
the series began filming in early 2022, though

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:04.110
no details on a release date were given at
that point.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:08.649
Fans speculated that the show would drop at
some point in 2023, and they were correct

00:01:08.649 --> 00:01:13.430
— as revealed at Netflix's Tudum event in
Brazil, we now know that all eight episodes

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:18.350
of "One Piece" Season 1 will premiere on August
31, 2023.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:19.731
What's One Piece about?

00:01:19.731 --> 00:01:23.079
"One Piece" takes place in a fictional world
not too dissimilar to our own, though much

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:25.400
of the planet's surface is covered by water.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.400
The only continent is called the Red Line,
a narrow strip of land that goes all the way

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.390
around the globe.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:34.950
Also circling the globe is the Grand Line,
a stretch of ocean that's known for its rapidly

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.210
changing weather and dangerous sea life.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:41.570
When they cross, these two lines split a huge
body of water known as the Blue Sea into four

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.600
quadrants: the East Blue, the West Blue, the
North Blue, and the South Blue.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.270
There are many, many islands in the Blue Sea,
some of them safer than others — this is

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.979
a world full of pirates, after all.

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:57.490
The story follows the Straw Hat pirates, named
after the hat worn by their optimistic captain

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:59.027
Monkey D. Luffy.

00:01:59.027 --> 00:02:03.320
It's Luffy's dream to find the legendary treasure
known as the One Piece, which was hidden by

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.930
the famed pirate Gol D. Roger before his execution
at the hands of the World Government.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:12.330
Whoever finds it will be proclaimed the new
Pirate King, and Luffy won't stop until that

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:13.501
title is his.

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:17.750
He knows he can't do this alone, however,
so he sets about putting a crew together and

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.266
making a name for himself.

00:02:19.266 --> 00:02:23.290
This is all covered in the East Blue saga,
which — going by the promotional material

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.490
and casting news — is what the first season
of Netflix's "One Piece" will be based on.

00:02:28.113 --> 00:02:32.790
The lead role of Monkey D. Luffy is being
played by a young actor named Iñaki Godoy,

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.810
building on a filmography that thus far consists
predominantly of projects made in his home

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.330
country of Mexico.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:42.629
In an interview with Decider, Godoy revealed
that he started reading the manga and watching

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:46.890
the anime after winning his "One Piece" role,
mainly so he could get a grasp on Luffy's

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:51.860
bizarre physicality — after eating a Devil
Fruit, his body has the properties of rubber.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:52.517
He said,

00:02:52.517 --> 00:02:55.230
"How can I translate this rubber-man into
live action?

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:59.110
In those cases, I really used the anime and
manga for reference and I literally tried

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.230
to replicate the way this guy fights."

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.674
He continued in detail,

00:03:02.674 --> 00:03:07.099
"When he throws a punch and he takes it back,
he puts his hand on his bicep or his shoulder.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:09.680
So, if he does that, I should do that too,
right?

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.959
And when he's about to throw a punch, sometimes
he lifts up his leg.

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:16.239
I just tried to look for those little details
in the original product."

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:20.390
Rounding out Luffy's Straw Hat Pirates crew
are Mackenyu, the son of martial arts movie

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.569
legend Sonny Chiba, as the master swordsman
Zoro.

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:29.033
Emily Rudd, who played Clara in Amazon's "Hunters,"
appears as the enigmatic thief and navigator Nami.

00:03:29.033 --> 00:03:32.846
Taz Skylar, who you might remember as Walt
in TNT's "The Lazarus Project," plays the

00:03:32.846 --> 00:03:34.900
womanizing chef Sanji.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:40.409
Rounding out the Straw Hats is Jacob Romero
Gibson, who played AJ Delajae in OWN's "Greenleaf,"

00:03:40.409 --> 00:03:42.500
as the skilled marksman Usopp.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:47.130
Supporting additions to the cast include Peter
Gadiot as Luffy's idol Red-Haired Shanks,

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:52.439
McKinley Belcher III as the villainous fishman
Arlong, Ilia Isorelýs Paulino as ruthless

00:03:52.439 --> 00:03:56.599
pirate Alvida, and Jeff Ward as the clown-dressing
captain Buggy.

00:03:56.599 --> 00:04:01.730
We'll also see Steven Ward as the fearsome
swordfighter Mihawk, Chioma Umeala as Nami's

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:08.219
adoptive older sister Nojiko, and Morgan Davies
as cabin boy and future Navy captain Koby.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.549
It has been confirmed that the first two episodes
of "One Piece" Season 1 are directed by Marc

00:04:12.549 --> 00:04:16.760
Jobst, who has some history with shows about
pirates, having helmed a Season 4 episode

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:17.760
of "Black Sails."

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:23.720
The Starz series, which ran for four seasons
between 2014 and 2017, is a prequel to "Treasure

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.820
Island" and is about pirates seeking riches
while evading the British government.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:31.300
The themes are very similar, though there's
no superpowers in "Black Sails."

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:34.800
But that's not to say that Jobst doesn't have
experience in that area.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.480
Jobst is perhaps best known for his work on
Netflix's hit "Daredevil" series, most notably

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.919
helming the Season 3 opener "Resurrection."

00:04:41.919 --> 00:04:46.040
He also directed a couple of episodes of "Luke
Cage," meaning he has a shared history with

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:50.640
"One Piece" co-showrunner Matt Owens — Owens
was a staff writer and executive story editor

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:51.740
on the Marvel show.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:56.030
Jobst is also known for directing episodes
of "The Witcher" and "Jupiter's Legacy," both

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:00.000
Netflix shows, so he's clearly trusted by
the higher-ups at the streamer.

00:05:00.879 --> 00:05:05.020
Marty Adelstein, whose company Tomorrow Studios
helped get the live-action "One Piece" series

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:09.940
off the ground, is an executive producer on
the show, but fans will no doubt be more excited

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:14.020
to learn that "One Piece" creator Eiichiro
Oda is also producing.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:18.130
When Oda was first approached about adapting
his long-running manga into a live-action,

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:22.080
English language series back in 2016, he had
some doubts.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.160
Even after agreeing to the project, he had
some major reservations, as there were some

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.160
difficulties to begin with.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.430
In a lengthy Instagram post, Oda wrote,

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:34.800
"We obviously come from very different cultures
so, when it comes to entertainment, we have

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.520
different codes, skill sets, and aims.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.900
Sometimes it could be frustrating for both
sides.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.250
It felt like, 'We're all trying to get to
the same place so how come we're not on the

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:44.250
same wavelength?'

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:48.100
There was even a time when I thought, 'Is
a foreign production even possible?!'"

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.850
Luckily, they were all able to get on to the
same page, taking the series in a direction

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:57.169
that Oda was happy with — the creator revealed
that he had, and still has, the final say

00:05:57.169 --> 00:05:59.517
when it comes to what goes into the Netflix
show.

00:05:59.517 --> 00:06:00.157
He wrote,

00:06:00.157 --> 00:06:02.541
"They've promised they won't launch until
I'm satisfied.

00:06:02.541 --> 00:06:06.750
They're burning with passion, and I've reminded
everyone involved that this should be fun."

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:07.800
Returning voice cast

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.660
In what was perhaps the most exciting announcement
at Anime Expo 2023, it was confirmed that

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.020
the voice cast from the long-running "One
Piece" anime series will be playing their

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:19.849
respective characters in the Japanese dub
of Netflix's live action show.

00:06:19.849 --> 00:06:23.730
In a clip shared at the convention, Iñaki
Godoy revealed that he recently took a trip

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:27.820
to Tokyo to meet the original voice of Luffy,
Mayumi Tanaka.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:33.230
Tanaka has been playing Luffy since 1999,
so, as a fan, Godoy was understandably very

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:35.270
excited to meet her.

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:41.819
"Luffy and you inspire me to be the best version
of myself that I can be.

00:06:41.819 --> 00:06:44.199
I'm just so honored to meet you."

00:06:44.199 --> 00:06:48.039
It seems like Tanaka was proud of of the new
Luffy actor, too, since she presented him

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:50.130
with a straw hat as a gift, telling him,

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:51.830
"I leave this hat with you.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.940
Bring it to me someday once you've become
a great pirate."

00:06:55.099 --> 00:06:59.520
Showrunners Matt Owens and Steven Maeda wrote
the first and last episode of Netflix's "One

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.979
Piece," entitled "Romance Dawn" and "Worst
in the East."

00:07:02.979 --> 00:07:06.889
Maeda is perhaps best known as one of the
producers of "Lost," but he has plenty of

00:07:06.889 --> 00:07:10.290
other noteworthy credits to his name, including
"Lie to Me" and "Helix."

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:16.539
He also wrote more than 40 episodes of "The
X-Files" between 2000 and 2002.

00:07:16.539 --> 00:07:21.100
Matt Owens was a staff writer and executive
story editor on "Daredevil," but that's not

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:25.620
the only Marvel show he's been involved in
— he was also a writer on "Luke Cage," "Agents

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.780
of SHIELD," and "The Defenders."

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:32.010
The other writers involved in "One Piece"
have worked on shows like the animated "Harley

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:36.270
Quinn" series, "Supergirl," and "Umbrella
Academy," so it seems like the humorous, super-powered

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:39.381
antics of the manga and anime will be in good
hands.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:43.870
When Netflix released the second trailer,
the streamer shared some words from "One Piece"

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:44.960
creator Eiichiro Oda.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:49.310
In a note that was posted to Twitter, Oda
said that he fully expected fans of the source

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:53.349
material to highlight the differences between
the manga and the live-action show.

00:07:53.349 --> 00:07:56.759
"After the launch, I'm sure I'll hear about
some people pointing out how this character

00:07:56.759 --> 00:08:00.970
is missing or that scene is omitted, or this
bit is different from the manga.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:04.960
But I'm sure they'll come from a place of
love, so I intend to enjoy even those comments!"

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.770
As Oda predicted, most of the early comments
were good natured, with fans on the whole

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:13.163
accepting that some changes need to be made
when it comes to creating something in real life.

00:08:13.163 --> 00:08:14.369
One Reddit user wrote,

00:08:14.369 --> 00:08:16.919
"I definitely see some changes to the plot.

00:08:16.919 --> 00:08:22.159
Arlong's crew seems to show up at the Baratie,
Buggy's crew seems to have an actual circus

00:08:22.159 --> 00:08:27.000
going on, plus it seems like the Going Merry
encounters Garp's ship at one point.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:28.639
Everything still looks really good though!"

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:32.669
You'll never please everyone when it comes
to adapting a beloved manga series, but the

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:36.080
live-action "One Piece" seems to have plenty
of support from the fandom when it comes to

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.752
making tweaks — of course, having Oda on
board certainly helps.

